        "summary": "Anneka says to explicitly show you care about the person’s success, be direct, and prepare feedback that is specific, constructive, and collaborative.",
        "summary_citation_ids": [
          "S1",
          "S2"
        ],
        "sections": [
          {
            "heading": "Make your supportive intent clear",
            "content": "Tell the person directly that you care about them and are giving the feedback because you want them to succeed and reach their potential. Reinforce that message with your body language; don’t just assume they understand your intent. Anneka notes that you can’t fully control how someone responds, but you can control what you say and how you present yourself.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S1"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Be direct, prepared, and collaborative",
            "content": "Avoid passive-aggressive hints. State what the person is doing or how they are perceived, and offer concrete changes they can make. Prepare in advance to frame the feedback so it resonates without feeling like an attack; explain why the behavior isn’t working or is being perceived poorly, and give examples—including, when useful, experiences of your own. Let them ask questions and brainstorm solutions together, rather than leaving them to figure it out alone.",
            "citation_ids": [
              "S2"
            ]
          }
        ],
